 {"id": "EXAMPLE-APP-001", "title": "[P1] Prove the versatility thesis: ship ONE application built directly on the memory store (a task board), with no separate backend", "status": "complete", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-04T17:51:08Z", "reasoning": "Bane's framing 2026-11-04: DuckBrain's point is VERSATILITY - 'how close can you run the database and application server' - so that a user or an agent builds the feature they want directly on the store instead of chasing a separate app backend. He points at a peer project (punkrecords) that ships a task manager and kanban board as proof of the idea. DuckBrain today has the PRIMITIVES and no DEMONSTRATION: 'duckbrain tables declare' writes a SUPA-6 schema.json, the generic REST layer serves CRUD with filters/order/limit plus PATCH/DELETE by primary key, per-table grants are enforced on every verb, the SUPA-2 serializer audits every mutation, X-Durability is on every write, and SSE publishes the change feed - all verified on a scratch daemon 2026-11-04 (declare widgets -> insert -> ?qty=gte. -> PATCH ?pk=eq. -> DELETE ?pk=eq. -> audit row with contentHash). What does not exist is a single shipped example an outsider can run to see that building an app on the store is real. Without one, the versatility claim is a thesis, not a product.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) A task-board application lives in the repo (e.g. examples/taskboard/) whose ENTIRE persistence is DuckBrain: tables declared in schema.json (e.g. boards, columns, cards with ids, ordering, timestamps), all reads through GET /api/ns/:ns/tables/:table with query params, all writes through POST/PATCH/DELETE including primary-key addressing. No second datastore, no SQLite, no JSON file of its own - prove it with 'grep -r' in the app dir showing zero alternate persistence. (2) It runs against a scratch daemon from a documented one-command start, and a committed transcript/screenshot shows: create board -> create column -> create card -> MOVE a card between columns (ordering change visible) -> filter cards by assignee -> delete a card -> the resulting rows read back correctly. (3) Permissions are exercised, not decorative: a token WITHOUT write grant can read the board but gets 403 on POST/PATCH/DELETE, and the denial appears in the namespace audit with reason table_grant. (4) A README section states in plain steps how a user declares their OWN tables and gets the same CRUD+grants+audit surface - the point is that the pattern generalises, not that this app exists. (5) The change feed is shown being used: subscribe to the namespace, move a card in another shell, and the event arrives. (6) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit as any new suite; full suite green before landing.", "updated_at": "2026-11-05T19:08:55Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "9c35865e60edca6b768cfd12c12fa8dbbdac84274df2bcb4e84e05ea94448360", "evidence": [{"run_id":"bc5cd660","ts":"2026-11-05T19:08:55Z"}]}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "claimed by harden-loop 2026-11-05T19:03:01Z; worktree wt/EXAMPLE-APP-001\n\nlanded 654b3c5b (ancestor of origin/main; examples/taskboard in its tree). AC1: grep -rniE 'localStorage|sessionStorage|indexedDB|sqlite|writeFile|appendFile|createWriteStream' over public/, serve.mjs, verify.mjs -> no output; serve.mjs only fs.readFile of static assets. AC2: docs/transcript.txt steps 1-6,10 (board->columns->cards, move via PATCH ?pk=, ordering change visible, assignee=eq. filter, DELETE, read-back). AC3: steps 7-8 reader token GET 200, POST/DELETE 403 FORBIDDEN, audit row {principal taskboard-reader, op tables.write, outcome denied, reason table_grant}. AC4: README 'Declare your own tables and get the same surface'. AC5: step 9 SSE event for a card moved by a second client, no polling. AC6: test-counts.json 256/2131 in the same commit; pnpm test:run 256 files/2131 tests green; tsc --noEmit clean; tier1 judge bc5cd660 PASS. UI verified in headless chrome: DOM renders 3 columns + 4 cards + healthy pill (docs/screenshot.png). Enabling fix: CORS middleware ran AFTER auth so a browser preflight got 401 with no ACAO — now CORS+preflight run before auth, X-API-Key/PATCH allowed, X-Durability/X-Total-Count exposed, pinned by src/cli/http-cors-example-app-001.test.ts (4/6 fail on pre-fix code). Second defect found and fixed here: replay.ts same-window insert+delete (row REPLAY-BIRTH-DEATH-001). NOT proven: no restart of the production daemon (:3000 untouched, scratch :28778 only); demo tokens are scratch keys exposed via /config.json (documented limit); no multi-user login flow."}
 {"id": "SEC-AUTHZ-001", "title": "[P1] 17 of 37 live API tokens are ADMIN because 'roles' is optional — the grant model is bypassed for half the fleet", "status": "pending", "priority": "P1", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-04T18:06:06Z", "reasoning": "Found by the 2026-11-04 bakeoff while scoring our own tree (ours/duckbrain.json, row auth.legacy-token-admin-equivalence = WARNS_NEVER_REFUSES). src/auth/roles.ts:73-77: effectiveRoles() returns [\"admin\"] when principal.roles is undefined, documented as backward compatibility for the pre-SUPA-4 token shape. Measured against the live store (~/.duckbrain/auth.json, 37 keys): 17 have no roles field, and 37 of 37 carry no tableGrants. Consequence: (a) a token minted by any older tooling is a full admin on every namespace, silently — there is no signal at token creation and none at use; (b) the per-table grant layer SUPA-4 claims to enforce has never been exercised in production, so the only evidence it works is a scratch-daemon probe, not a deployment; (c) my own earlier report to the owner ('grants bite — verified: write-only grant GET 403, read-only DELETE 403') was true of the MECHANISM on a scratch daemon and misleading about the live system, which is the exact failure mode the boundary probe was supposed to prevent. Not a theoretical risk: an agent given a read-only key that was minted without a roles field can write and delete.", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) Make the default explicit and safe: a token with no roles field must not be admin — either default to a least-privilege role, or refuse to authenticate it with a clear error naming the token and the migration command. Prove with a test that a roles-less token cannot write to a namespace it was not granted, and that the failure names the reason. (2) Migrate the live population, do not just change the default: enumerate the 17 roles-less keys, assign each an intended role (ask the owner where intent is unknown — do NOT auto-grant admin to preserve the status quo), and re-count to 0 roles-less keys, printing before/after counts. (3) Exercise the grant layer for real at least once per role class: create one read-only token with explicit roles + explicit tableGrants and show GET 200 / PATCH 403 / DELETE 403 against a scratch daemon, with the denial visible in the namespace audit carrying reason table_grant. (4) Add a startup WARN (metrics + log) counting tokens without roles so the population cannot silently regrow; expose it as a number, not prose. (5) Update SUPA-4's row text: 'enforced' currently means 'the code can refuse', and it must also say how many live principals are actually subject to the rule.", "updated_at": "2026-11-06T16:41:03Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "2ce285ea317189b9c9d714fbfa56626de1fd6547c5c9eca091fad2c802e5386c"}, "worker_status": "pending", "commit_hash": null, "guard_result": null, "ci_result": null, "worker_summary": null, "foreman_note": "harden-loop 2026-11-06: skipped by this fire as owner-gated in practice, not dispatchable code work. Criterion (1) (a roles-less token must not be admin) cannot be landed before criterion (2) (assign an intended role to each of the 17 live roles-less keys) without taking out half the fleet: the same 17 tokens authenticate live agents today, so flipping the default or refusing them is a production outage until the owner states intent per key. Next fire should either (a) land (1)+(3)+(4)+(5) behind an explicit owner-approved migration window, or (b) wait for Bane's per-key intent list; do NOT auto-assign roles.", "blocked_reason": "OWNER INPUT REQUIRED: per-key intended role for 17 live roles-less tokens in ~/.duckbrain/auth.json (AC-2 says ask the owner where intent is unknown). Flipping the AC-1 default before that migration breaks every fleet agent holding one of those keys."}
