 {"id": "REST-UPSERT-NUMERIC-PK-001", "title": "[P1] PUT upsert 422s on every schema.json-declared table with a numeric primary key; string keys work — and the tests only ever use string keys", "priority": "P1", "status": "complete", "complexity": 2, "created_at": "2026-11-07T04:24:10Z", "created_by": null, "reasoning": "Found 2026-11-07 by exercising the surface on a scratch daemon after REST-ATOMICITY-001 was closed complete (e18fb3c2). Same daemon, same principal, same table family: PUT /api/ns/cards/tables/texty/c1 with a varchar primary key -> HTTP 201; PUT /api/ns/cards/tables/cards/8 with an integer primary key -> HTTP 422 {'error':\"Column 'id' (float64): expected a finite JSON number, got \\\"8\\\" (table 'cards')\"}. Root cause is visible in src/tables/operations.ts: typedPkValue() returns the RAW STRING whenever col.declaredType !== undefined (the SUPA-6 declared path), with a comment saying the declared validator will normalise it — but that validator requires a real JSON number for a numeric column, so the value the row is assembled with (row[col.name] = pkTyped) fails its own declaration. The legacy .table.json path takes JSON primitives and is unaffected, which is why this survived: the new suite declares its fixtures with string primary keys (customers/c1, customers/c3, invoices/x1 in src/http/routes/tables-rest-atomicity.test.ts) so no test crosses the numeric path. Consequence: the upsert verb — the headline of the atomicity work — is unusable on exactly the declaration format ADOPT-DECLARED-001 pushed everyone toward, and an app with integer ids silently cannot replace a row. Second, related: 'tables declare --column id=integer' persists {'type':'float64'} in schema.json, so an integer declaration becomes a float (and the error names float64 for a declared integer).", "acceptance_criteria": "(1) PUT upsert on a schema.json-declared table with a numeric primary key returns 201 (insert) then 200 (replace) with the resulting row, and the persisted row carries a JSON number, not a string. (2) The coercion is driven by the DECLARED type, not by 'the validator will sort it out': numeric declared types (float64/int64-as-number) become numbers, canonical-string types (timestamp, bytes, int64-as-string) stay strings. (3) Prove the falsifier that exists today: a test that PUTs an integer primary key on a declared table and fails on the pre-fix code with the exact 422 above. (4) Cover both pk shapes in the same suite — varchar (currently the only shape) and numeric — so this cannot regress silently. (5) Decide and record what '--column id=integer' should persist (float64 vs an integer type) and make the error message name the DECLARED type the user wrote, not the storage type. (6) test-counts.json bumped in the same commit; full suite green.", "updated_at": "2026-11-07T05:59:52Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "ed1bfbe43705b191c1a5a090f565549828468a9311f4e0dc7706b665182efd99", "evidence": [{"run_id":"8ca5a32d","ts":"2026-11-07T05:59:52Z"}]}, "foreman_note": "FIXED 887622ec (ancestor of origin/main; declaredPkValue present in origin/main's src/tables/operations.ts). typedPkValue now coerces by DECLARED type: float64 -> finite JSON number, boolean -> true/false, int64/timestamp/string/bytes/json stay canonical text; legacy .table.json path untouched. LIVE PROOF on a scratch daemon running the rebuilt dist (port 28840, declared table items with an integer pk): PUT /items/7 -> 201 {outcome:inserted,row:{id:7,label:first}}; PUT /items/7 again -> 200 {outcome:updated}; GET ?pk=eq.7 -> the row; the stored JSONL line is {\\\"id\\\":7,...} i.e. a JSON number; PUT /items/abc -> 400 \\\"Primary key 'id' is a float64 and requires a finite number\\\" (was 422). REGRESSION: 2 new cases in tables-rest-atomicity.test.ts; control with the fix reverted fails both (422 vs 201/400). Suite 281 files / 2389 tests green at the judged commit, tsc 0 errors, test-counts.json synced in the same commit. ALSO IN THIS COMMIT: declare help states integer/double persist as declared float64 (bigint for exact 64-bit), and SUPA-6 documents that a row policy applies to every authenticated principal INCLUDING admin — behaviour the RLS suite already asserted and the spec did not state.", "commit_hash": "887622ec", "ci_result": "SKIP", "guard_result": "PASS"}
 {"id": "HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-008", "title": "[P1] Legacy row-image audit records (pre-cb55f40c) fail ChangeRecordSchema — 134 of 196 production namespaces and 17,346 commits cannot replay, contradicting cb55f40c's replay-without-migration claim", "priority": "P1", "status": "pending", "complexity": 3, "created_at": "2026-11-07T04:30:35Z", "created_by": null, "reasoning": "Measured 2026-11-07 by the HARDEN-DUCKBRAIN-007 census (reports/changelog-corrupt-census-2026-11-07.md + artifact JSON beside it): 135 of 196 git-backed production namespaces fail derivation on at least one first-parent commit; 134 of them (17,346 commits) fail with class malformed-change-record, and every sampled instance is the SAME legacy shape — an accepted audit line carrying a full 'row' image and no 'contentHash'/'targetPath'/'tombstone'/'schemaVersion' (e.g. namespaces/infra _audit/current.jsonl:91, commit 8d586478, 2026-09-17; namespaces/anime:1; namespaces/default:79). Root cause: cb55f40c (SCHED-GAP-1574, 2026-10-06) replaced 'row: z.unknown()' with a REQUIRED 64-hex 'contentHash' in ChangeRecordSchema while isChangeRecordCandidate deliberately keeps promoting legacy marker rows ('legacy row-image records keep validating through the other markers'), so the two halves disagree and legacy lines are graded malformed instead of accepted. Its commit message states 'pre-existing ledgers replay without migration' — the code does not do that. Impact: any replay/seek that reaches a pre-2026-10-06 commit fails closed with CHANGELOG_CORRUPT (full-history derivation, old cursors, ordinal validation, and the realtime warm-up path deriveCommitChanges uses); the 61 clean namespaces are those with no pre-10-06 accepted record. Live probe 2026-11-07 on a scratch repo with the exact production shape: THREW CHANGELOG_CORRUPT 'is malformed: targetPath: expected string, contentHash: expected string, key: expected record, tombstone: expected boolean, schemaVersion: expected number'. Options: (a) union record schema — accept a legacy record when it carries a full 'row' and no contentHash, use the row image directly (pre-1574 semantics) and still enforce declared key columns; (b) backfill contentHash into committed ledgers = history rewrite, owner-gated, NOT this row. (a) is code-only and preserves the hash guard for hash-format records.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A legacy row-image record derives: a fixture seeded with the exact production shape (namespaces/infra commit 8d586478 _audit/current.jsonl:91 — ts/ns/table/op/principal/outcome/seq/row, no contentHash/targetPath/tombstone/schemaVersion) makes deriveCommitChanges return one change whose row is the record's row image and whose key follows the declared key columns; paste the call and the returned DerivedChange. 2) Falsifier: the same probe on pre-fix code throws CHANGELOG_CORRUPT 'is malformed' (paste both outputs side by side; the probe lives in the test file as a fixture so it re-runs). 3) The hash guard is NOT weakened: a hash-format record with a fabricated contentHash still throws; a legacy record missing a declared key column still throws; a legacy delete record without a row image (or with row: null) still throws. 4) Census delta on a frozen COPY of the two largest legacy namespaces (scheduler, pokemon-global — copied into a scratch root, never the production tree): the malformed-change-record class count drops to 0 and no namespace the 2026-11-07 census reports clean becomes corrupt; paste both census summaries. 5) No production namespace is rewritten, no git history rewritten, no schema migration applied to committed ledgers; the legacy branch is read-only tolerance. 6) Full suite green and test-counts.json updated in the same commit.", "updated_at": "2026-11-07T04:30:35Z", "detail": {"fingerprint": "f52bd0b28200c7dbfef7407ceacb40ea0474bed0a0531a2df45c4ea001f1d69c"}, "foreman_note": null, "commit_hash": null, "ci_result": null}
